     "t": "Before elective LDLT an observation interval with sustained tumor control is documented; a proposed 3-month local default applies and any departure needs explicit MDT rationale.",
     "why": "Consensus-informed local default. The protocol labels 3 months a conservative proposal, not a validated threshold. Its source, the 2012 international consensus, describes considering an observation period such as 3 months to judge tumor behavior but does not make it mandatory, and no comparison of intervals is cited; longer observation can also add dropout risk. Track recurrence and pre-transplant dropout by observation interval among LDLT recipients.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §8 Living Donor Liver Transplantation (LDLT)",
     "refs": [],
     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "Bevacizumab is stopped at least 28 days before elective surgery and not restarted for at least 28 days after major surgery and wound healing; transplant timing may need a longer individual interval.",
     "why": "Extrapolated from the drug label. The 28-day minimum comes from the US prescribing information for elective surgery; the protocol states it is not a proven safe interval for liver transplantation and cites no transplant-specific study. Track wound, bleeding and vascular complications in recipients with recent anti-VEGF exposure.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §6 Bridging and treatment-to-transplant interface (Systemic exposure and operative safety); same text in high-risk pathway document §4.1",
     "refs": [],
     "related": null
