    {
     "t": "mTOR-containing CNI minimization may be considered individually for renal or oncologic-risk reasons (everolimus not before day 30); it is not offered as proven prevention of recurrence.",
     "why": "Conditional (weak, level 3) guideline suggestion; the randomized trial missed its primary endpoint. In SiLVER (525 randomized recipients) recurrence-free survival did not differ significantly; benefit appeared only over the first 3–5 years and in exploratory, unpowered subgroups. The AASLD/AST suggestion is confined to HCC within Milan. Track recurrence, renal function and mTOR toxicity by regimen.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §10.2 mTOR Inhibitor Consideration",
     "refs": [],
     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "For recurrence after transplant, multikinase inhibitors (sorafenib, lenvatinib) are the usual first systemic choice; checkpoint inhibitors are exceptional salvage after transplant-oncology review.",
     "why": "Local convention. The protocol cites no source or comparative post-transplant trial for the first-choice statement. Avoiding checkpoint inhibitors rests on one retrospective series of 52 recipients (32 with HCC) in which seven had rejection and five died of it; pre-transplant safety estimates do not transfer to this setting. Track response, rejection, graft loss and survival for every systemic regimen used after transplant.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §11.3 Management of Recurrence",
     "refs": [],
     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "For selected oligorecurrence after transplant, complete resection is considered; intrahepatic recurrence is treated with ablation, radiation or intra-arterial therapy chosen case by case.",
     "why": "Selected retrospective series. The protocol states that the observational survival advantage of resection is strongly selection-confounded and that the local modalities are not supported as equivalent by transplant-specific comparative trials; part of the radiation evidence comes from non-transplant cohorts. Track morbidity, graft function and survival after each salvage treatment.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §11.3 Management of Recurrence",
     "refs": [],
     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "Program audit targets: five-year post-transplant overall survival above 70% and five-year cumulative HCC recurrence below 15%, both provisional and pending committee approval.",
     "why": "Local benchmarks, not validated thresholds. The cited systematic review describes five-year survival above 70% in selected Milan cohorts with recurrence commonly 10–20%, but calculates no pooled estimate or quality trigger, and its reported recurrence range is not consistently below 15%. A result outside target prompts case-mix and process review, not an automatic finding of preventable failure.",
