  "hehe": {
   "code": "HEHE",
   "name": "Hepatic epithelioid hemangioendothelioma",
   "limited": [
    {
     "t": "In the lower-risk cohort, more than 120 days before transplant is preferred only to observe disease behavior when safe; no wait is imposed with progression, failing liver function or symptoms.",
     "why": "Single-registry association, confounded by indication. In the ELTR registry a wait of 120 days or less was linked to recurrence, but rapid transplantation selects aggressive or symptomatic disease, so the protocol states there is no evidence that deliberate delay reduces recurrence; the registry also measured waiting from listing, not diagnosis or staging. It is a center safeguard, not an OPTN rule. Track recurrence and dropout by observation interval.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §5.1 Guidance-aligned lower-risk institutional cohort; decision 'Assess benefit and risk'",
     "refs": [
      {
       "key": "E-score",
       "label": "HEHE-LT · cohort",
       "dialog": true
      }
     ],
     "related": {
      "label": "HEHE-B1 · Observation and the need for transplantation",
      "href": "#high-risk-hehe-b1"
     }
    },
    {
     "t": "Treated hilar nodes, prior progression, systemic therapy needed for control, uncertain rupture or ≤120 days' observation place a patient in the high-risk cohort, needing separate oncology and allocation-ethics approval.",
     "why": "Local construct built on one registry. Among 149 ELTR recipients, hilar-node invasion and a wait of 120 days or less were associated with recurrence, but the registry excludes patients who dropped out and its score relies on explant pathology. The protocol cites no outcome data for rupture history or prior systemic therapy as risk markers. Track outcomes by cohort and how often a pre-transplant risk feature is confirmed at explant.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §5 Risk-stratified cohorts (§5.1–§5.2)",
     "refs": [
      {
       "key": "E-score",
       "label": "HEHE-LT · cohort",
       "dialog": true
      }
     ],
     "related": {
      "label": "HEHE-B2 · Extrahepatic disease and vascular invasion",
      "href": "#high-risk-hehe-b2"
     }
    },
    {
     "t": "Macrovascular invasion excludes transplantation in both cohorts; the pathway permits no macrovascular-invasion cohort.",
     "why": "NLRB guidance and registry prognosis, made absolute locally. NLRB lists absence of macrovascular invasion as a condition for favorable exception review; the protocol turns it into a clinical exclusion. In the ELTR registry it carried the highest recurrence risk, but the registry holds only selected recipients, and the German S2k guidance treats it as not an absolute barrier to evaluation. Track referrals declined on this criterion and their outcomes.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §2 Mandatory guidance criteria and §6 Exclusion and deferral criteria; decision 'Stage and compare options'",
     "refs": [
      {
       "key": "N-hehe",
       "label": "NLRB · HEHE",
       "dialog": true
      },
      {
       "key": "E-score",
       "label": "HEHE-LT · cohort",
       "dialog": true
      },
      {
       "key": "dgvs_lt2024",
       "label": "DGVS S2k LT guidance",
       "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39250961/"
      }
     ],
     "related": {
      "label": "HEHE-B2 · Extrahepatic disease and vascular invasion",
      "href": "#high-risk-hehe-b2"
     }
    },
    {
     "t": "No neoadjuvant or bridging regimen is required; the sarcoma–transplant MDT chooses observation, ablation, embolization, radiation or systemic therapy case by case.",
     "why": "Expert consensus. The international EHE consensus supports active surveillance for stable disease and individual treatment choice. The only comparison cited is a 50-patient single-center series in which five-year survival from diagnosis did not differ by initial strategy; the groups were small and clinically selected, so this is not equivalence, and 14 of 25 patients first observed progressed. Track progression and dropout by pre-transplant strategy.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §7 Pretransplant treatment and stability",
     "refs": [
