  "hcc": {
   "code": "HCC",
   "name": "Hepatocellular carcinoma",
   "limited": [
    {
     "t": "Every patient is reviewed at multidisciplinary conference before listing, before bridging or downstaging starts, and at each restaging interval.",
     "why": "Guideline consensus, extended locally. NCCN supports multidisciplinary transplant-center assessment; reviewing before every treatment start and at each restaging (about 3-monthly while waiting) is the protocol's own frequency, and it cites no study of how often review is needed or what it changes. Track the interval from restaging to conference decision and how often review changes the plan.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §2 Multidisciplinary Team (restaging interval in §4.3 Restaging While on Waitlist)",
     "refs": [
      {
       "key": "NCCN-hcc",
       "label": "NCCN · HCC v2.2026",
       "dialog": true
      }
     ],
     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "After successful downstaging no institutional stability period is added; timing follows the allocation rules (imaging ≥4 weeks after the last treatment and the 6-month wait from the first exception request).",
     "why": "Local choice resting on allocation rules, not outcome data. The 2012 international consensus noted that at least 3 months of observation after downstaging was often recommended, but that was never a randomized optimization of observation time; waiting longer may expose aggressive biology but also adds dropout and decompensation risk. Track recurrence and dropout by time from successful downstaging to transplant.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §7.4 Observation Period",
     "refs": [
      {
       "key": "N-hcc",
       "label": "NLRB · HCC",
       "dialog": true
      },
      {
       "key": "H-policy",
       "label": "OPTN 9.5.I",
       "dialog": true
      }
     ],
     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "Before elective LDLT an observation interval with sustained tumor control is documented; a proposed 3-month local default applies and any departure needs explicit MDT rationale.",
     "why": "Consensus-informed local default. The protocol labels 3 months a conservative proposal, not a validated threshold. Its source, the 2012 international consensus, describes considering an observation period such as 3 months to judge tumor behavior but does not make it mandatory, and no comparison of intervals is cited; longer observation can also add dropout risk. Track recurrence and pre-transplant dropout by observation interval among LDLT recipients.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §8 Living Donor Liver Transplantation (LDLT)",
     "refs": [],
     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "Bevacizumab is stopped at least 28 days before elective surgery and not restarted for at least 28 days after major surgery and wound healing; transplant timing may need a longer individual interval.",
     "why": "Extrapolated from the drug label. The 28-day minimum comes from the US prescribing information for elective surgery; the protocol states it is not a proven safe interval for liver transplantation and cites no transplant-specific study. Track wound, bleeding and vascular complications in recipients with recent anti-VEGF exposure.",
