     "related": null
    },
    {
     "t": "If checkpoint-inhibitor therapy is used, a washout of at least 90 days precedes transplant; afterwards, liver tests are checked twice weekly for 4 weeks, then weekly to week 12, with biopsy for unexplained rises.",
     "why": "Protocol convention. The protocol calls the 90-day washout a convention, not a validated threshold: no interval has been shown to eliminate rejection, and rejection has been reported after longer intervals. Evidence is retrospective and largely HCC-derived (Moeckli 2025, VITALITY). The monitoring schedule is a local operational rule; pooled retrospective data (8 studies, n = 229) show 19% rejection with no reliable onset time. Track washout, rejection and graft loss.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §4.1 Systemic Therapy and §7.2 Maintenance; decision 'Plan pretransplant treatment'",
