    {
     "t": "Biopsy is reserved for atypical imaging, suspected malignant transformation, or when molecular subtype will change management, and targets the dominant or most suspicious lesion.",
     "why": "Guideline-consistent, with limited data for lesion choice. ACG notes that MRI cannot reliably identify β-catenin activation or separate HCA from well-differentiated HCC. Sampling the dominant lesion rests on one molecular cohort (Nault 2017): subtype differed between tumors in 21 of 73 patients with several sampled lesions, and the exon 3 mutation was in the largest tumor in 8 of 9 relevant cases. Track subtype by lesion sampled and biopsy-explant concordance.",
     "setting": "standard",
     "locator": "Standard pathway document §3 Diagnostic and multidisciplinary confirmation; decision 'Characterize risk'",
     "refs": [
